# Reproducing "factor of BA degree completion = -0.9568" (NLS SES)

**Answer: the Black-vs-White pair** (Black = numerator/lower completion, White = reference).
log OR = -0.9568 -> OR = exp(-0.9568) = **0.384**: Black respondents' odds of completing a BA
are ~38% of White respondents'.

## Data (data/nls_ses_processed.csv)
N = 8,773; 9 cols (8 documented + undocumented `Unnamed: 0` index). 0 missing values,
0 duplicate CASE IDs, 3 clean race labels (White 5,337 / Black 2,113 / Hispanic 1,323),
BA DEGREE COMPLETED stored as True/False (236 True = 2.690%). Only cleaning needed:
drop the index column, cast BA to 0/1.

| race | n | BA=1 | mean |
|---|---|---|---|
| White | 5,337 | 189 | 0.035413 |
| Hispanic | 1,323 | 19 | 0.014361 |
| Black | 2,113 | 28 | 0.013251 |

## Search: 14 estimator families, 1,404 distinct race-contrast statistics
unadjusted 2x2 (all 6 ordered directions) | race-only logistic with all 3 reference codings |
race + every one of the 32 covariate subsets of {SES, ABILITY, PERCENTILE, FAMSIZE, SEX} |
GLM vs hand-rolled IRLS | probit / cloglog links | Firth | two-race subsamples x 32 subsets |
one-race-vs-pooled-others | MH stratified by each covariate | within-sex / within-SES-quartile fits |
race x covariate interaction models (contrast at covariate mean) | coarsened (dichotomised/
quantile/log) covariates giving partial adjustment | outlier-trimmed samples.

**No specification reproduces -0.9568 exactly.** Best |delta| = 0.0011 (Black/White, crude
logOR inside the FAMSIZE<=4 subsample) - arbitrary filter, and per the calibration below it is
noise, not evidence.

